INT. COURTROOM 4B - NIGHT

A vast oak-paneled room, dark except for the red glow of an emergency exit sign. A power outage has stopped the clocks. Benches are empty. A stenographer's chair sits angled away from the bench. The witness box stands open like an unlit stage.

JUDGE HARRIET BALOGUN-REYNOLDS (60s, black robe, flashlight under her chin) sits at the bench, peering through reading glasses at a conference phone placed before her. Its tiny green light is the only steady glow in the room.

HARRIET
(to the room)
For the record, we are proceeding under emergency conditions.

The phone crackles.

ATTORNEY QUIGLEY (V.O.)
(through the speaker, tinny)
Your Honor, I'm in the parking structure. My signal is poor. Can you hear me?

HARRIET
Barely, Counselor.

QUIGLEY (V.O.)
Your Honor, I move for a continuance.

HARRIET
Denied.

QUIGLEY (V.O.)
Your Honor, the power is out.

HARRIET
The law is not.

She sets down her flashlight. It rolls. She chases it with two fingers, mutters.

HARRIET (CONT'D)
(to herself)
Forty years. Never a continuance for weather.

QUIGLEY (V.O.)
I didn't catch that.

HARRIET
You weren't meant to.

She rubs her eyes. The sign hums. The silence stretches.

HARRIET (CONT'D)
Counselor, your client has been waiting nine months for this hearing.

QUIGLEY (V.O.)
He's waiting in the car.

HARRIET
Bring him up.

QUIGLEY (V.O.)
Your Honor?

HARRIET
Stairs. Eleven flights. By candlelight if necessary.

QUIGLEY (V.O.)
Your Honor, he's seventy-eight.

HARRIET
I know how old he is.

A pause. A long breath crackles on the line.

QUIGLEY (V.O.)
We'll be there in twenty minutes.

HARRIET
(softly)
Take thirty.

She switches off the flashlight. The red sign glows. She folds her hands, a small figure in a great black robe, and waits.
